<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sheikh ul-Islam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sheikh ul-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was the title of the chief religious authority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, who headed the learned hierarchy of Islamic scholars. The holder issued legal opinions (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a>) on matters of law and state policy. Accounts of the Ottoman Empire treat the office as an important part of Ottoman governance, linking the state to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> religious legitimacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sheikh-ul-islam.jpg" alt="Sheikh ul-Islam" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mhsheikholeslami · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shaykh_al-Isl%C4%81m_in_different_languages.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
